<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saichō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">767–<a href="/years/822">822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Dengyō Daishi, Hirono</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saichō was a Japanese Buddhist monk credited with founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> school, posthumously titled Dengyō Daishi. Fully ordained at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a>, he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, where his community became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>, and in 804 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> to study the Tiantai school. On his return he made Enryaku-ji the centre of Tendai, joining Tiantai meditation, study, precepts and ritual with esoteric practices. He sought an ordination based on the bodhisattva precepts rather than the traditional Vinaya; the Buddhist establishment protested, and his request was granted only seven days after his death in 822. His school laid the ground for later Pure Land and Zen traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saicho.jpg" alt="Saichō" width="330" height="544" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:最澄像_一乗寺蔵_平安時代.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Saichō was born in 767 in Ōmi, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, with the given name Hirono. Family tradition traced his ancestors to emperors of Eastern Han China, for which no positive evidence exists, though the region had a large Chinese immigrant population. At thirteen he became a disciple of the monk Gyōhyō, took tonsure at fourteen and received the name Saichō. By twenty he had received the full precepts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji</a>. A few months later he abruptly retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, for a reason that is unknown, and wrote his Ganmon, a prayer of vows not to leave the mountain or take part in worldly affairs until he had attained wisdom.</p><p>Monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and elsewhere gathered round him, and the community grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enryaku-ji">Enryaku-ji</a>. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 795, and because Hiei lay to its north-east, a direction thought dangerous in Chinese geomancy, his presence there was believed to protect the city and brought him to the court's notice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a> granted his petition to study Tiantai doctrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Because he could read Chinese but not speak it, he took the disciple Gishin. The four-ship diplomatic mission of 803 was turned back by winds, and on the second attempt two ships sank, but his reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningbo">Ningbo</a> in 804. At Tiantai Mountain Daosui, the seventh Tiantai patriarch, was his chief teacher for about 135 days. Saichō copied texts and, at Yuezhou, received esoteric initiation, though it is unclear which transmission he received.</p><p>He returned to Tsushima in 805 after about eight months, and in the first month of 806 the court recognised his school and allowed two annual ordinands, one on the esoteric course and one on the Tiantai course based on Zhiyi's Mohe Zhiguan.</p><p>He had met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kukai">Kūkai</a>, who had gone to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on a longer mission, and the two cooperated for a time. Saichō helped Kūkai to give the esoteric initiation at court and supported the gift of Takaosan-ji to him. Their relationship later deteriorated over Kūkai's secretive approach to esoteric practice. Saichō also debated the Yogācāra scholar Tokuitsu over whether all beings can attain Buddhahood, and defended the view that they can.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 822 Saichō asked that Hiei monks be ordained under the Bodhisattva Precepts of the Brahma Net Sutra instead of the traditional Vinaya, a petition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> establishment protested. He wrote the Kenkairon in defence. He died on 26 June 822 at fifty-six, and the court granted the request seven days later, with the ordination platform built in 827. His ordination model became standard in many schools that grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a>, among them Jōdo-shū, Rinzai and Sōtō Zen.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saichō” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Japan, <b>Saichō</b> (767 to 822) brought Tiantai as the Tendai school.</blockquote><a href="/work/lotus-sutra">Lotus Sutra · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Kūkai and <b>Saichō</b> were later ordained there too.</blockquote><a href="/work/todai-ji">Tōdai-ji · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Tendai, brought from China by the monk <b>Saichō</b>, rested on the Lotus Sutra and taught that enlightenment was open to “every creature”; its monastery on Mount Hiei, above the capital, grew close to the court and taught great reverence for the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shiga district. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hiei">Mount Hiei</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.07,135.84&amp;year=802" aria-label="Mount Hiei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Saichō"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4019 4506h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4019 4506h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.07,135.84&amp;year=802">What was here in 802 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
